{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; tail -100 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc.claude-fable-5-1.stdout-r4.log; sed -n '445,690p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v445\"","aggregated_output":" M docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\n M docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\n M docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\n?? docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\nBoth r3 findings are closed in `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md`, now revision r4. Only docs changed, and nothing is staged or committed.\n\n## Finding 1: repairs starved by a full shared backlog (HIGH)\n\n- **Separate capacities.** The fresh queue and the repair queue no longer share one bound. The repair queue has its own capacity that fresh writes can never occupy.\n- **Explicit admission turn.** Repair admission runs before fresh admission in every step and does not depend on it.\n- **Per-publication batch.** A repairing publication holds at most 64 addresses in the repair queue at once. Sixteen repairing publications times 64 equals the repair queue's capacity, so every publication's batch always fits.\n- **Cycle lifecycle.** A cycle opens when due, offers every retryable failed blob, and closes when each has been admitted once and settled. The next delay is computed from the close.\n- **Stated guarantee.** Every due repair receives a replicate request within a bounded number of steps and ticks, whatever the fresh queue holds.\n- **L1 acceptance.** A new test keeps the fresh queue full without pause while an older failed row is admitted, requested and acknowledged. It fails if the repair waits for the fresh queue to drain. A second test covers publications with more failures than one batch.\n\n## Finding 2: publications awaiting a first report were unbounded (MEDIUM)\n\n- **New bound.** At most 16 publications may await their first report. One more announcement does not wait and displaces nothing. Every blob of the new publication fails at once with a new retryable reason, `publications-full`, its first report is made in that step, and it continues under repair.\n- **Narrowed claim.** `:max-repairing` now explicitly bounds repairing publications only. Section 5.5.2 states the full retention bound: live publications, ledger entries, both queues and outstanding requests.\n- **L1 acceptance.** The dead-network test now asserts the two queue bounds separately and repairing publications only. A separate retained-ledger test covers the open bound, the total of ledger entries, and many open publications sharing one queued address.\n\nThe limits table and the failure vocabulary carry the new settings and the new reason.\n   445\tname assertion of section 6 in the same index.\n   446\t\n   447\t### 5.5 Replication: the ledger, the backlog, repair and the result\n   448\t\n   449\tThe node's store handle no longer asks the DHT to replicate at the moment of\n   450\ta put. A put inserts into `:local`, records the address on the node's ledger\n   451\tring, and returns the local verdict. Oversize is still refused before the\n   452\tlocal insert. `step` owns everything after that. No round waits, as before.\n   453\t\n   454\t#### 5.5.1 The publication ledger\n   455\t\n   456\tA **publication** is the set of distinct addresses put between two\n   457\tannouncements, closed by the announced manifest. The node keeps one ledger\n   458\tper live publication: every address of the publication, each in exactly one\n   459\tof three states.\n   460\t\n   461\t| State | Meaning |\n   462\t|---|---|\n   463\t| `waiting` | No outcome yet: the address is queued or its request is outstanding, for the first time in this publication. |\n   464\t| `sent` | A `/sent` fact arrived while this publication held the address. Never leaves this state. |\n   465\t| `failed` | The last outcome was not `/sent`. Carries that reason. May become `sent` by a later repair. |\n   466\t\n   467\t- The ledger is **complete and fixed**: `:blobs` is its size and never\n   468\t  changes. Repair updates this ledger; it never creates another publication\n   469\t  for the same manifest.\n   470\t- A publication is **live** from its announcement until it is retired.\n   471\t  It is retired when it is `:acknowledged`, when its repair ends (5.5.4),\n   472\t  or when the node closes.\n   473\t- **After the first report, every blob is `sent` or `failed`.** A failed\n   474\t  blob whose repair try is queued or outstanding stays `failed`, with its\n   475\t  last reason, until a `/sent` arrives. So in every event of a publication\n   476\t  `:sent + (count :failed) = :blobs`.\n   477\t\n   478\t**Shared addresses.** One address has at most one queue entry and at most\n   479\tone outstanding request on the node, whatever number of live publications\n   480\thold it.\n   481\t\n   482\t- When an address's request settles, the outcome is written to the ledger\n   483\t  of **every live publication** that holds the address as `waiting` or\n   484\t  `failed`: `/sent` makes it `sent` in each; a failure makes it `failed`\n   485\t  with that reason in each.\n   486\t- A publication announced while an address it holds is already queued or\n   487\t  outstanding joins that one attempt.\n   488\t- Nothing else is shared. An outcome recorded before a publication was\n   489\t  announced does not count for it: its address is admitted and requested\n   490\t  again. A write is idempotent by content address.\n   491\t\n   492\t#### 5.5.2 The backlog\n   493\t\n   494\tA node holds two FIFO queues of addresses awaiting a replicate request: the\n   495\t**fresh** queue, for a publication's first pass, and the **repair** queue,\n   496\tfor retries.\n   497\t\n   498\t- **Admission** of fresh addresses happens in `step`, when the ledger ring\n   499\t  is read: each put address is appended to the fresh tail in put order.\n   500\t- **Two capacities, never shared.** The fresh queue holds at most\n   501\t  `:max-backlog` addresses (default 4096). The repair queue has its own\n   502\t  capacity, `:max-repairing × :repair-batch` (16 × 64 = 1024 by default),\n   503\t  which fresh writes can never occupy. No amount of fresh writing denies a\n   504\t  repair its place, and no amount of repair denies a fresh write its place.\n   505\t- **A full fresh queue.** A fresh address that arrives when the fresh queue\n   506\t  is full is **not admitted**: it is `failed` at once with reason\n   507\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`. Its local copy stays. Nothing is evicted to\n   508\t  make room.\n   509\t- **The repair queue is never full for a publication's batch.** A repairing\n   510\t  publication holds at most `:repair-batch` (default 64) addresses in the\n   511\t  repair queue at once, and at most `:max-repairing` publications repair,\n   512\t  so the capacity above always has room for every repairing publication's\n   513\t  batch (5.5.4).\n   514\t- **Open publications are bounded.** At most `:max-open` (default 16)\n   515\t  publications may await their first report. An announcement that would\n   516\t  make one more does not wait and displaces nothing: every address of the\n   517\t  new publication is `failed` at once with reason\n   518\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`, none is `waiting`, its first report\n   519\t  is made in that step, and it continues under repair. An address it shares\n   520\t  with an attempt already in progress is `failed` like the rest, and takes\n   521\t  that attempt's outcome when it settles, by the shared-address rule of\n   522\t  5.5.1.\n   523\t- **What a node retains** is therefore bounded by composition data alone:\n   524\t  at most `:max-open + :max-repairing` live publications, each ledger at\n   525\t  most the ledger ring's capacity in addresses; at most `:max-backlog`\n   526\t  fresh and `:max-repairing × :repair-batch` repair queue entries; at most\n   527\t  `max-pending-writes` requests outstanding. `:max-repairing` bounds\n   528\t  repairing publications only.\n   529\t- **Release.** Each step appends replicate requests while the number\n   530\t  outstanding is below the DHT's `max-pending-writes`. The node never has\n   531\t  more outstanding than that bound, so its own writes never draw `/busy`.\n   532\t- **Fairness between the queues.** While the repair queue is non-empty,\n   533\t  `:repair-slots` (default 16) of the outstanding bound are reserved for\n   534\t  it: fresh requests hold at most `max-pending-writes − repair-slots`\n   535\t  outstanding, repair requests at most `:repair-slots`. While the repair\n   536\t  queue is empty, fresh requests may use the whole bound. Neither queue can\n   537\t  starve the other, and repairs never take more than their share from a new\n   538\t  publication.\n   539\t- **Order.** Each queue is strictly FIFO. Fresh order is put order, so an\n   540\t  older publication's first pass precedes a newer one's. No publication is\n   541\t  prioritized.\n   542\t- **Outcomes.** One request has one outcome. `/sent` is `sent`. Every\n   543\t  `/unacknowledged` reason is `failed` with that reason. There is no\n   544\t  immediate second try: a retry is a repair (5.5.4).\n   545\t- **A solo or non-publishing node holds no backlog.** Every put is `failed`\n   546\t  at admission with `/solo` or `/unpublished`.\n   547\t- **A lost ledger-ring entry** (more puts in one window than the ring\n   548\t  holds) or a facts gap reports `:publication-unknown`, as today, and that\n   549\t  publication is not live.\n   550\t\n   551\t#### 5.5.3 The result and the events\n   552\t\n   553\t```clojure\n   554\t{:dao.space.dht/event :published | :republished\n   555\t :manifest m\n   556\t :result :acknowledged | :partial | :unacknowledged\n   557\t :blobs n            ; the ledger's size; constant for the publication\n   558\t :sent s             ; ledger entries sent\n   559\t :peers p            ; fewest peers any sent blob reached; absent when s = 0\n   560\t :failed [{:address a :reason r :peers k} ...]   ; every entry not sent\n   561\t :repairing? b       ; true while the node will retry this publication\n   562\t :ended reason}      ; only when repair ended without acknowledgement\n   563\t```\n   564\t\n   565\t| `:result` | Condition, over the whole ledger | What it promises |\n   566\t|---|---|---|\n   567\t| `:acknowledged` | Every blob sent. | Every address of the publication was handed to at least `ack-peers` peers. |\n   568\t| `:partial` | The manifest blob sent; at least one other blob not. | Peers can find the publication. The addresses under `:failed` are held and served by the publisher only. |\n   569\t| `:unacknowledged` | The manifest blob not sent. | Nothing beyond the local copy. `:failed` still lists every blob not sent. |\n   570\t\n   571\t- The result is always computed over the **whole ledger**. A blob sent in\n   572\t  the first pass and a blob sent by a repair count alike. A publication\n   573\t  whose manifest was sent in the first pass and whose rows were sent by\n   574\t  repair is `:acknowledged`.\n   575\t- `:failed` is complete, in address order.\n   576\t- `:acknowledged?` is removed, with no compatibility path.\n   577\t\n   578\t**Which event, when.** A publication produces:\n   579\t\n   580\t| Event | When | Times |\n   581\t|---|---|---|\n   582\t| `:published` | The **first report**: every blob has left `waiting`. | Exactly once. |\n   583\t| `:republished` | A repair changed the `:result` (to `:partial` or to `:acknowledged`), or repair ended. A change that ends repair is one event. | Zero or more; at most two result changes and one end. |\n   584\t\n   585\t- `:repairing?` is true in an event exactly when the result is not\n   586\t  `:acknowledged` and repair has not ended.\n   587\t- A repair cycle that changes no result produces **no event**. A\n   588\t  publication that never improves is silent after its first report.\n   589\t- **Final acknowledgement** is the one `:republished` (or the first\n   590\t  `:published`) whose `:result` is `:acknowledged`. Nothing follows it.\n   591\t- `:ended` is one of `:dao.space.dht/terminal` (every remaining failure is\n   592\t  terminal), `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`, `:dao.space.dht/displaced` (5.5.4).\n   593\t\n   594\t#### 5.5.4 Automatic repair (owner decision 5: \"Automatic retry while open\")\n   595\t\n   596\tA publication whose first report is not `:acknowledged` is repaired by the\n   597\tnode, in the background, for as long as the node is open.\n   598\t\n   599\t- **What is retried.** A `failed` blob whose reason is **retryable**:\n   600\t  `/too-few-peers`, `/busy`, `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`,\n   601\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`. A blob that is `sent` is never sent\n   602\t  again.\n   603\t- **What is not.** A **terminal** reason is never retried: `/solo`,\n   604\t  `/unpublished`, `/oversize`, `/absent`, `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. A solo\n   605\t  or non-publishing node therefore repairs nothing: its composition cannot\n   606\t  change while it is open.\n   607\t- **A repair cycle.** Each repairing publication has a due reading, in the\n   608\t  node's own ticks. At a step whose reading has reached it, a cycle\n   609\t  **opens**: it offers every retryable failed blob the publication holds at\n   610\t  that moment, in address order.\n   611\t- **The admission turn.** In every step, repair admission runs **before**\n   612\t  fresh admission, and does not depend on it. For each publication with an\n   613\t  open cycle, oldest publication first, offered blobs not yet admitted are\n   614\t  appended to the repair tail, in address order, until the publication\n   615\t  holds `:repair-batch` addresses in the repair queue. An offered blob that\n   616\t  is already queued or outstanding through another publication counts as\n   617\t  admitted. The cycle **closes** when every blob it offered has been\n   618\t  admitted once and has settled.\n   619\t- **Every due repair is requested.** The repair queue's capacity is its\n   620\t  own, a publication's batch always fits, and `:repair-slots` requests are\n   621\t  reserved while the repair queue is non-empty. A request ends by its fact\n   622\t  or by the DHT's `ack-ticks` deadline. So each offered blob of each\n   623\t  repairing publication receives a replicate request within a bounded\n   624\t  number of steps and ticks, whatever the fresh queue holds and however\n   625\t  long fresh writes continue.\n   626\t- **Pacing.** The first cycle is due `:repair-ticks` (default 30000) after\n   627\t  the first report. The next is due after the cycle closes: after a cycle\n   628\t  in which no blob of the publication was sent, the delay doubles, up to\n   629\t  `:repair-max-ticks` (default 600000); after a cycle in which one was, it\n   630\t  returns to `:repair-ticks`. A tick gap makes a cycle late, never wrong.\n   631\t- **Bounds.** At most `:max-repairing` (default 16) publications repair at\n   632\t  once. When one more needs repair, the oldest is retired with\n   633\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/displaced`. With the bounds of 5.5.2, a node that\n   634\t  can reach nobody retains a bounded number of ledgers, queue entries and\n   635\t  requests, and says nothing after each first report beyond one\n   636\t  displacement event per publication retired.\n   637\t- **Repair ends** in exactly four ways:\n   638\t  1. the result becomes `:acknowledged` (the final event; no `:ended`);\n   639\t  2. no retryable failure remains and the result is not `:acknowledged`\n   640\t     (`:ended :dao.space.dht/terminal`). When this already holds at the\n   641\t     first report, that `:published` event carries it and\n   642\t     `:repairing? false`;\n   643\t  3. `cancel!` (5.5.5);\n   644\t  4. displacement.\n   645\t\n   646\t  And silently when the node closes: `close!` discards every ledger and\n   647\t  both queues and reports nothing. Local copies are durable. A node opened\n   648\t  again over the same directory repairs nothing from before; the ledger is\n   649\t  process state and is not rebuilt.\n   650\t- **A manual nudge.** `(dao.space.dht/retry! node manifest-address)` makes\n   651\t  that publication's next cycle due now and resets its delay. It changes\n   652\t  no rule above and reports nothing itself. It is refused for a manifest\n   653\t  that is not repairing.\n   654\t- `busy?` is true while a first report is owed or any address is queued or\n   655\t  outstanding. A publication waiting for its next cycle does not make the\n   656\t  node busy.\n   657\t\n   658\t#### 5.5.5 Cancellation\n   659\t\n   660\t`(dao.space.dht/cancel! node manifest-address)` ends a live publication now.\n   661\tIt is refused for a manifest with no live publication.\n   662\t\n   663\tEvery ledger entry is settled for **this publication** at the moment of the\n   664\tcall:\n   665\t\n   666\t| Entry at the call | Becomes | The write itself |\n   667\t|---|---|---|\n   668\t| `sent` | Stays `sent`. | Done. |\n   669\t| `failed`, nothing outstanding | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`; the earlier reason is kept under `:was`. | Its queued repair entry, if any, is removed, unless another live publication holds the address. |\n   670\t| `waiting`, queued | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. | Its queue entry is removed, unless another live publication holds the address. |\n   671\t| `waiting` or `failed`, **request outstanding** | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. | The request cannot be withdrawn. It runs to its fact. |\n   672\t\n   673\t- **Cancellation does not wait.** The publication is reported by the next\n   674\t  step, as `:published` if its first report had not been made and as\n   675\t  `:republished` otherwise, with `:repairing? false` and\n   676\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/cancelled`, and it is retired. Its counts are\n   677\t  those of the table, so `:sent + (count :failed) = :blobs`.\n   678\t- **A late fact never changes a reported cancellation.** The fact of an\n   679\t  outstanding write that a cancelled publication held is applied to the\n   680\t  other live publications that hold the address, by the shared-address\n   681\t  rule, and to nothing else. The cancelled publication is retired and its\n   682\t  event is not amended.\n   683\t- **`cancelled` means not counted, not unsent.** A blob reported\n   684\t  `:dao.space.dht/cancelled` whose request was outstanding may have reached\n   685\t  peers. The result under-claims and never over-claims.\n   686\t- An address another live publication holds keeps its one queue entry and\n   687\t  its one request; that publication's accounting is untouched.\n   688\t- Nothing cancels a publication implicitly. A newer round does not\n   689\t  supersede an older one's writes.\n   690\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
