{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1170,1250p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1170; sed -n '1080,1098p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1080; git diff -- docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\"","aggregated_output":"  1170\t`docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` (section 10's event list). Acceptance:\n  1171\t- `load-index` is `load`; every existing index-load test passes with\n  1172\t  reasons asserted as data.\n  1173\t- A walk answering `:missing` fetches; `:complete` loads; `:invalid` fails\n  1174\t  without any fetch. A throwing walk fails with code\n  1175\t  `:dao.space.dht/walk-threw` and a walk answering another shape with\n  1176\t  `:dao.space.dht/walk-shape`; both codes are asserted equal on the JVM,\n  1177\t  Node and Dart, and no test compares `:text`.\n  1178\t- `request-get` answering `:busy` leaves the load `:loading` and it\n  1179\t  completes on a later step. A client that cannot submit fails the load\n  1180\t  `:unaskable` with the outcome keyword, the same value on all three hosts.\n  1181\t- **Pacing.** One round of 500 puts against acknowledging peers is\n  1182\t  `:acknowledged`; the number of outstanding replicate requests never\n  1183\t  exceeds `max-pending-writes` at any step, and no `/busy` fact is\n  1184\t  produced.\n  1185\t- **Sustained rounds.** Eight consecutive rounds of 500 puts (within\n  1186\t  `:max-backlog` in total), announced faster than they drain, are each\n  1187\t  reported once, in announcement order, all `:acknowledged`; the backlog\n  1188\t  returns to empty; `busy?` then answers false. A ninth and tenth round\n  1189\t  announced before any drain exceed the bound: their excess blobs are\n  1190\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full` in their first reports, which are not\n  1191\t  `:acknowledged`; repair then sends them and each round ends\n  1192\t  `:republished :acknowledged`.\n  1193\t- **The result.** A mesh that refuses the store of one chosen non-manifest\n  1194\t  blob reports `:partial` with exactly that address under `:failed`; one\n  1195\t  that refuses the manifest blob reports `:unacknowledged`. HEAD has moved\n  1196\t  in each case.\n  1197\t- **The invariant.** In every `:published` and `:republished` event of\n  1198\t  every test in this slice, `:sent` plus the count of `:failed` equals\n  1199\t  `:blobs`, and `:blobs` is the same in all events of one publication.\n  1200\t- **Repair reaches acknowledgement, from both originals.** A `:partial`\n  1201\t  original (manifest sent, one row refused): after the mesh heals and\n  1202\t  ticks pass `:repair-ticks`, exactly one `:republished` arrives with\n  1203\t  `:acknowledged`, `:sent` equal to `:blobs`, and the manifest's replicate\n  1204\t  request was issued once in total. An `:unacknowledged` original (manifest\n  1205\t  and rows refused): healing first the manifest's store, then the rest,\n  1206\t  yields `:republished :partial`, then `:republished :acknowledged`, and\n  1207\t  nothing after. A blob already `sent` is never requested again.\n  1208\t- **A peer that never accepts.** With every peer refusing or never\n  1209\t  answering stores, over sustained rounds and a long run of appended ticks:\n  1210\t  every first report is `:unacknowledged` with `:repairing? true`; **no\n  1211\t  event ever reports `:acknowledged`**; no `:republished` is produced\n  1212\t  except one `:ended :dao.space.dht/displaced` per publication beyond\n  1213\t  `:max-repairing`; the fresh queue never exceeds `:max-backlog` and the\n  1214\t  repair queue never exceeds `:max-repairing × :repair-batch`;\n  1215\t  outstanding requests never exceed `max-pending-writes`; repairing\n  1216\t  publications never exceed `:max-repairing`; the repair delay doubles to\n  1217\t  `:repair-max-ticks` and stays; no put ever waits; and `busy?` is false\n  1218\t  between cycles.\n  1219\t- **Retained ledgers stay bounded, measured separately.** Under the same\n  1220\t  dead network, with rounds announced faster than first reports arrive:\n  1221\t  publications awaiting a first report never exceed `:max-open`; the\n  1222\t  announcement beyond it is reported in the step that reads it, every blob\n  1223\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`, `:repairing? true`, displacing\n  1224\t  nothing open; live publications never exceed `:max-open +\n  1225\t  :max-repairing`; and the total of ledger entries the node retains, summed\n  1226\t  over live publications, stays at or below that count times the ledger\n  1227\t  ring's capacity through the whole run. A case with many open publications\n  1228\t  sharing one queued address asserts the same total while the queue holds\n  1229\t  one entry.\n  1230\t- **Fairness of requests.** With the repair queue full of failing addresses\n  1231\t  and a new round arriving, the new round's first pass holds at least\n  1232\t  `max-pending-writes − repair-slots` outstanding and is reported while\n  1233\t  repairs continue; repair requests never exceed `:repair-slots`\n  1234\t  outstanding; with no repairs queued, a fresh round uses the whole bound.\n  1235\t- **Repair under sustained fresh writes.** One early publication has one\n  1236\t  row refused and is `:partial`. The peers then heal for that row. From\n  1237\t  before its first cycle is due, and without pause, fresh rounds keep the\n  1238\t  fresh queue at `:max-backlog`, their excess `:backlog-full`. The early\n  1239\t  publication's failed row is admitted to the repair queue in the step its\n  1240\t  cycle opens, receives its replicate request within the reserved slots\n  1241\t  while the fresh queue is still full, and the publication is reported\n  1242\t  `:republished :acknowledged`, all while fresh writes continue. The test\n  1243\t  fails if the repair is admitted only after the fresh queue drains.\n  1244\t- **Batches.** A publication with more retryable failures than\n  1245\t  `:repair-batch` never ho